            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to give a gentle smile, then the Mandarin for peaceful inside."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: píqi",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "píqi — \"temper; temperament\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ bàba de píqi bǐ wǒ māma hǎo — My dad is better-tempered than my mum."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "píqi is built from pí 'the spleen' and qì 'breath', both inherited from Middle Chinese, bjie and khj+jH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which explains the first, after an older book, as the organ of earth that helps the stomach. Temper as the breath of the spleen is the usual explanation, not one the dictionary gives."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying how it was."
